duce the exponent 47 modulo 12:\n\n\\[47 \\div 12 = 3 \\text{ remainder } 11 \\implies 47 \\equiv 11 \\mod 12 \\implies 2^{47} \\equiv 2^{11} \\mod 13.\\]\n\nNext, we need to find \\(2^{11} \\mod 13\\):\n\n\\[2^{11} = 2048.\\]\n\nTo find \\(2048 \\mod 13\\), we perform the division:\n\n\\[2048 \\div 13 = 157 \\text{ remainder } 7 \\implies 2048 \\equiv 7 \\mod 13.\\]\n\nSo, \\(2^{47} \\equiv 7 \\mod 13\\).\n\nNow, we need to find \\(3^7 \\mod 13\\):\n\n\\[3^7 = 2187.\\]\n\nTo find \\(2187 \\mod 13\\), we perform the division:\n\n\\[2187 \\div 13 = 168 \\text{ remainder } 3 \\implies 2187 \\equiv 3 \\mod 13.\\]\n\nSo, \\(3^7 \\equiv 3 \\mod 13\\).\n\nFinally, we combine these results:\n\n\\[2^{47} \\cdot 3^7 \\equiv 7 \\cdot 3 \\equiv 21 \\mod 13.\\]\n\nTo find \\(21 \\mod 13\\), we perform the division:\n\n\\[21 \\div 13 = 1 \\text{ remainder } 8 \\implies 21 \\equiv 8 \\mod 13.\\]\n\nTherefore, the remainder when \\(2^3 \\cdot 4^5 \\cdot 6^7 \\cdot 8^9\\) is divided by 13 is \\(\\boxed{8}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{8}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"number_theory:134"}
